    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Poly3D has been widely used in the Rock Fracture Project group over the past eight years to investigate fault mechanics including fault slip distributions, fault interactions and perturbed stress fields around faults. Today it is apparent that such a computer program has a broad range of applications to subsurface problems in the petroleum industry.\r\n\r\nIn this contribution, we present new functionality and applications for Poly3D in addressing critical problems for industry: (i) Two kinds of gravity loading have been implemented, lithostatic and hydrostatic loads; (ii) We show how the traction free surfaces (discontinuities) can be used to compute stress around modeled wellbores, voids, salt domes, and slipping bedding interfaces; (iii) An inverse module has been developed in order to compute slip distribution from displacement data such as structure contour maps; (iv) Finally, we compute paleo-stress/strain from observed 3D fault geometry and slip distribution.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
